.TH "SKARN-BASELINE" "1" "2026-06-23" "skarn 0.36.0" "Skarn Manual"
.SH NAME
skarn\-baseline \- Triage into a baseline (audit); accept a false positive
(accept)
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBskarn\fR \fBbaseline\fR \fIaudit|accept\fR \fIfile\fR [<fingerprint>] [options]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Interactively triage findings into a committed baseline (audit), or record a
false\-positive fingerprint (accept).
.PP
Interactive baseline triage. audit walks each new finding and records a
true/false\-positive decision with a reason into the committed baseline;
accept records a single false\-positive fingerprint non\-interactively. audit
runs the same scan check runs and requires a license the same way, any tier
including the free one, and it exits 7 before any scan when none is usable;
accept scans nothing and needs no license. The audit scan is scoped by the
same filters as check. A false positive is suppressed on every later scan. A
finding labeled a true positive is recorded and keeps firing until the
credential is rotated, and those are the same labels the real\-time guard
honors. A true\-positive label also overrides comment suppression, so a
confirmed leak on a commented line keeps gating. A true positive wins over a
false positive on the same fingerprint wherever the two meet, in one file or
across the member files of an org baseline directory, so one member cannot
silence a leak another member confirmed. A finding that carries a secret is
fingerprinted by that secret, so its acceptance follows the secret across
sessions and machines. A finding with no secret, such as a behavioral
prompt\-poisoning finding, is fingerprinted by the session it fired in, so
accepting it silences that rule in that session only and the same rule in any
other session keeps firing. To accept a finding the guard flagged, use `skarn
guard accept <fingerprint>`: it takes the fingerprint straight from the
guard's audit record and defaults to the personal baseline the guard reads. An
audit's decisions are merged into the file as it stands when the audit ends,
under the baseline lock, and never downgrade a true positive recorded
meanwhile; an audit whose every decision was overtaken that way leaves the
file untouched and counts those decisions as skipped. When the lock cannot be
taken (a directory, a link, or an unopenable file at <file>.lock), audit
refuses before it shows the first finding, so no decision is typed and then
lost. Every baseline write lands through one atomic save that is readable by
its owner only on macOS and Linux, and a write whose sibling .lock file cannot
be opened or created is refused with nothing written.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-reason\fR \fItext\fR
Reason recorded with an accepted finding (audit prompts for one; accept takes
it as a flag).
.TP
\fB\-\-identity\fR \fIid\fR
Override the accepted\-by identity stamped on the decision; defaults to
$SKARN_IDENTITY.
.TP
\fB\-\-cli\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict the audit scan to one assistant's sessions. One of: claude, gemini,
codex, cursor, copilot, kimi, grok, grokbot, antigravity, opencode, cline,
copilot\-code.
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIpath\fR
Restrict the audit scan to matching projects: a comma\-separated list of names
or paths, matched case\-insensitively against a session's project display
name, its canonical root path, or a parent directory of that root. A session
read from a Compliance API export carries no project, so any \-\-project value
excludes it.
.TP
\fB\-\-session\fR \fIid\fR
Audit a single session by id.
.TP
\fB\-\-rules\fR \fIpath\fR
Load a custom TOML rules file for the audit scan in addition to the bundled
rules. A rule pattern whose compiled form exceeds 16 KiB is refused like any
pattern that fails to compile, so skarn exits 6 without auditing; an allowlist
pattern over that bound is skipped, so its suppression does not apply.
.TP
\fB\-\-hours\fR \fIn\fR
Audit scan window in hours; 0 means no time limit. The default is 720.
.TP
\fB\-\-severity\fR \fIlevel\fR
Only audit findings at or above this severity. One of: low, medium, high,
critical. The default is medium.
.TP
\fB\-\-user\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict the audit scan to matching users: a comma\-separated list of user ids
or email addresses, where an id matches exactly and an email address matches
case\-insensitively. Only a session read from a Compliance API export carries
either, so on a machine without an export, or without the Enterprise license
that reading one needs, every \-\-user value matches nothing.
